-- title:
--   Fermat-ring FFT multiplication recovers the negacyclic convolution of the pieces
-- statement:
--   Let $n\ge 1$, $k,Q,M\in\mathbb N$ with $2^kQ=n$, and write $F=2^n+1$, $K=2^k$. Assume
--   $$2\,(2^k)^2\,(2^M)^2 < 2^{n-1}.$$
--   Then for all integers $x,y$, with $\hat x = \mathrm{difZ}(F,2Q,k,\mathrm{wtZ}(F,Q,0,\mathrm{pcs}(M,K,x)))$ and $\hat y$ defined likewise,
--   $$\mathrm{liftL}\big(n,k,Q,K,\ \mathrm{ditZ}(F,n,2Q,k,\ \mathrm{pmulZ}(F,\hat x,\hat y))\big) = \mathrm{ncZ}\big(K,\ \mathrm{pcs}(M,K,x),\ \mathrm{pcs}(M,K,y)\big).$$
--   In words: cutting $x$ and $y$ into $K$ signed $M$-bit pieces, twisting by powers of $2^Q$, applying the forward FFT, multiplying pointwise mod $F$, applying the inverse FFT, untwisting, scaling by $K^{-1}$ and lifting to the symmetric range yields exactly (as integers, not only mod $F$) the negacyclic convolution
--   $$\mathrm{ncZ}(K,a,b)_m = \sum_{i+l=m} a_i b_l - \sum_{i+l=m+K} a_i b_l,\qquad 0\le m<K.$$
--
--   This is the algebraic heart of the Schönhage–Strassen correctness proof: the convolution theorem for the Fermat-ring transform with root $2^{2Q}$ and twist $2^Q$ (whose $K$-th power is $2^n\equiv-1$), together with the size bound guaranteeing that the coefficients $|\mathrm{ncZ}_m|\le K\,2^{2M}$ are recovered exactly from their residues.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** A purely mathematical statement on lists of integers; `difZ`, `ditZ`, `wtZ`, `pmulZ`, `liftL` are the reference functions that the item-language procedures are proved to compute, `pcs` uses signed (sign of $x$) truncating pieces, and `%` is the canonical nonnegative residue.
-- source:
--   A. Schönhage, V. Strassen, Schnelle Multiplikation großer Zahlen, Computing 7 (1971), 281–292 (main theorem: O(n log n log log n) multitape TM multiplication). Lemma `NLogN.ss_lift` of the Lean development NLogN/SSCorrect.lean (lines 17–79), written for the prove2.me integer-multiplication campaign.

